From 530e65c54ba24e2c52fca01c637bb466cf3dff71 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Heinemeier Hansson <david@hey.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 22:22:01 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: Use the performance governor in performance

Both methods of the cpufreq driver, the boost switch on Apple Silicon and
the caps on Snapdragon X and on Apple Silicon without boost frequencies,
left the governor at schedutil in every profile. schedutil raises
the frequency only as a task's tracked utilisation grows, over tens of
milliseconds, so bursty work such as a web browser runs most of its
bursts at low clocks even with no cap at all.

Speedometer 3.1 in an incognito Chromium window, in performance:

- Snapdragon X2 Elite (ASUS Zenbook A16, X2E-88-100) on AC: 23.1 +/- 3.1
  under schedutil, with the efficiency cores averaging 1.2 GHz and the
  prime cluster 1.9 GHz over the run, and 45.4 +/- 2.8 under the
  performance governor, against 48 on Windows on the same machine.
  Pinning the browser to the prime cores under schedutil only reached
  30.1, and Google's own Chrome build scored 28.2, so neither core
  placement nor the browser build accounts for the gap.
- Apple MacBook Neo (A18 Pro), which has no boost frequencies and so
  uses the caps method: 28.8 +/- 3.8 under schedutil and 34.4 +/- 2.3
  under the performance governor, matching a second pair of runs.

With either method, switch each policy to the performance governor in
performance and back to schedutil in balanced and power-saver. The caps
method does it in the same pass that writes the caps, and the boost
method after writing the switch, over every policy present, so new and
returning policies and resume are covered the same way. A policy that
doesn't offer both governors keeps its own. A failure to switch is
reported but doesn't fail the profile change, since the caps or the
boost switch still tell the profiles apart. When the daemon stops, the governor is set back to
schedutil, along with lifting the caps, so the CPUs aren't left at full
clock.

Apple Silicon with boost frequencies hasn't been measured, but runs the
same schedutil, so it gets the same governor as the Neo rather than a
split by model.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
